<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19868403</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2128232</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE ORMATION OF VACUOLES DUE TO BACILLUS TYPHOSUS IN THE CELLS OF TISSUE CULTURES OF THE INTESTINE OF THE CHICK EMBRYO</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lewis</surname><given-names>Margaret Reed</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1c014e0N0x3c6be70">From the Department of Embryology of Carnegie Institution of Washington, Baltimore.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>29</day><month>2</month><year>1920</year></pub-date><volume>31</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>293</fpage><lpage>311</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>16</day><month>11</month><year>1919</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1920, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The introduction of <italic>Bacillus typhosus</italic> into the hanging drop of a tissue culture of the intestine of a chick embryo leads to the rapid vacuolation of the cells of the tissues which comprise the growth of the cultures. The cells of the connective tissue, the endodermal membrane, the mesothelium, and also the clasmatocytes exhibit the ability to ingest <italic>Bacillus typhosus</italic> in the cultures. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>